# Build output.
/target/
**/target/

# Trunk's output for the web bundle. The versioned artifact is the Pages
# deployment and the mirror `pages.yml` pushes to `n1` (SPEC.md §16.3), not a
# local build.
/crates/cluster-web/dist/

# Binaries `images.yml` copies to the repository root so a Containerfile can
# COPY them. They are build output that happens to land here, and committing one
# would put a stale binary in the image build's path.
/cluster-health
/cluster-updater
/cluster-ctl
/devcontainer-agent

# Installer media and its checksums, built by `promote.yml` and published as
# release artifacts (§12.1). The checksum is the root of trust *in the release*;
# a copy in the tree would be a second source for it.
/iso/

# The Pages artifact `pages.yml` unpacks before mirroring it to `n1`.
/bundle/

# Editor and OS noise.
.DS_Store
*.swp
*~
.idea/
.vscode/

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Deliberately NOT ignored. Each of these looks like something a repository
# would ignore, and each is committed on purpose.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# `generated/` is rendered from `model/` and **committed**. It is diff-gated by
# `cargo xtask check-render`, which is what makes R1 cover infrastructure and not
# only documentation (§7.2): a reviewer sees the diff, and a hand-edit fails the
# gate. Ignoring it would delete the gate.
#
# `CONFORMANCE.md` is generated from `model/ids.toml` and committed, for the same
# reason and by the same rule (R1).
#
# `results/` is measurement output --- the only irreplaceable artifact the
# cluster produces (§5.6). `lv_data` is a single copy with no replica and no
# off-site target, so git *is* the backup, and committing it makes provenance a
# property of the same history that produced the image it was measured on.
#
# `Cargo.lock` is committed: the resolved version of every dependency is part of
# what `cargo deny` gates (R6), and a lock file regenerated per machine would
# make that gate a statement about whoever last ran it.
